Test the file wire against a scripted peer; fix four faults it found

A scripted 9P shell-files peer now drives the client through every B6c
behavior: EAGAIN retries of the same submit after progress or the backoff,
never in the refusing pass; Submitted before the submit reply and in the
final read before EOF; disconnect classification; both EALREADY branches;
refusal continuation and refusal after Submitted; exact write counts;
foreign epochs and kinds; object reads past msize, the cap probe, qid and
generation mismatches, undisclosed feeds and supersession; the catalog and
indicator families; the close scan stopping at a record full decoding
refuses; a Submitted and the submit error in one drain, either order; and a
clunk the pipeline cannot queue.

Faults it found and this fixes: replies drained in a pass did not count as
progress, so a request queued by a reply waited for the next call; an
EAGAIN retry could go in the refusing pass once an event was consumed; a
housekeeping clunk on a failed pipeline replaced the real error, while on a
live pipeline an unqueueable clunk now fails closed instead of leaking the
fid; and a submit error drained ahead of its Submitted misjudged custody,
so a drain now buffers its events before judging other replies. Event
intake and the close scan share one full decoder.
